      concepts: [
        { h: "Scarcity, choice and opportunity cost", b: "<p>Resources (the <strong>factors of production</strong>: land, labour, capital, entrepreneurship) are limited while wants are unlimited, so societies must choose <em>what</em>, <em>how</em> and <em>for whom</em> to produce. Every choice carries an <strong>opportunity cost</strong> - the value of the next best alternative forgone.</p>" },
        { h: "Production possibilities curve (PPC)", b: "<p>The PPC shows the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce with full and efficient use of resources and given technology.</p><ul><li>Points <strong>on</strong> the curve: productive efficiency. Points <strong>inside</strong>: unemployment/inefficiency. Points <strong>outside</strong>: currently unattainable.</li><li>Concave (bowed out) shape = increasing opportunity cost because resources are not equally suited to all uses.</li><li><strong>Outward shift</strong> = economic growth (more/better resources, technology). Movement from inside to the curve = actual growth using idle resources.</li></ul>" },
        { h: "Economic systems and the circular flow", b: "<p>Free market (price mechanism allocates), planned (government allocates) and mixed economies. The <strong>circular flow of income</strong> links households and firms; <em>leakages</em> (saving, taxes, imports) and <em>injections</em> (investment, government spending, exports) change the size of the flow.</p>" },
        { h: "How economists approach the world", b: "<p><strong>Positive</strong> statements can be tested with evidence; <strong>normative</strong> statements contain value judgements. Economists use models built on <em>ceteris paribus</em> assumptions, test them against data and refine them. Nine key concepts run through the course: scarcity, choice, efficiency, equity, economic well-being, sustainability, change, interdependence, intervention.</p>" },
      ],
